          %title
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A Schwartz-Bruhat function is a certain type of complex-valued function on a general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+compact+space"} locally compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorff
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+abelian+group"} abelian group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwartz+space"} Schwartz function
            on a space given as a finite product of copies of the real line, of the circle, and a finitely generated abelian group.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p The notion of Schwartz-Bruhat function is constructed in stages that parallel developments in the general structure theory of locally compact (Hausdorff) abelian groups.
          %p
            Recall the notion of
            %em compactly generated topological group
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            as a group. The structure of a compactly generated abelian Lie group is well-known: it is a product of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi m
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \times \mathbb{R}^m \times \mathbb{Z}^n
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            (thus of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mi p
                  %mo ×
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^p \times F
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is a finite abelian group and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T = \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}
            is a circle group). These are often called
            = succeed "." do
              %em elementary Lie groups
          #def1.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              be an elementary Lie group of type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi m
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \times \mathbb{R}^m \times \mathbb{Z}^n
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              is a compact abelian Lie group. A
              %em Schwartz-Bruhat function
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} infinitely differentiable function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: G \to \mathbb{C}
              that is
              = succeed ":" do
                %em rapidly decreasing
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is uniformly bounded in the
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              = succeed "-variables," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mo ∀
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                        %mo ∈
                        %msup
                          %mi ℕ
                          %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo ∀
                      %mrow
                        %mi β
                        %mo ,
                        %mi γ
                        %mo ∈
                        %msup
                          %mi ℕ
                          %mi m
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo ∃
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi K
                          %mrow
                            %mi α
                            %mo ,
                            %mi β
                            %mo ,
                            %mi γ
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %munder
                        %mi sup
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi j
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∈
                          %msup
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mi n
                          %mo ×
                          %msup
                            %mi ℝ
                            %mi m
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %msup
                          %mi j
                          %mi α
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mi β
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi γ
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi j
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mo &lt;
                      %msub
                        %mi K
                        %mrow
                          %mi α
                          %mo ,
                          %mi β
                          %mo ,
                          %mi γ
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\underset{\alpha \in \mathbb{N}^n}{\forall}\;\;
                    \\underset{\beta, \gamma \in \mathbb{N}^m}{\forall}\;\; \underset{K_{\alpha, \beta, \gamma} \gt 0}{\exists}\;
                    \\left(
                    \\underset{(j, x) \in \mathbb{Z}^n \times \mathbb{R}^m}{sup} {\Vert j^\alpha x^\beta \partial_{\gamma} f(x, j)  \Vert} \lt K_{\alpha, \beta, \gamma}
                    \\right)
            %p
              using the usual notations for multi-indices
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo ,
                      %mi β
                      %mo ,
                      %mi γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha, \beta, \gamma
          %p
            Next, any locally compact abelian group is canonically a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+colimit"} filtered colimit
            of the system of its open compactly generated subgroups and open inclusions between them. In particular, any abelian Lie group is canonically a filtered colimit of its open elementary Lie subgroups. In fact, an abelian Lie group is of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi m
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times \mathbb{R}^m \times T^p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a discrete abelian group. We may reckon
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as a filtered colimit of its finitely generated subgroups
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\alpha
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi m
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^m \times T^p
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi α
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi m
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\alpha \times \mathbb{R}^m \times T^p
          #def2.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em Schwartz-Bruhat function
              on an abelian Lie group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              is a continuous function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: G \to \mathbb{C}
              that is supported on an open elementary Lie subgroup
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi H
                    %mo :
                    %mi H
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f|_H: H \to \mathbb{C}
              is Schwartz-Bruhat in the sense of Definition
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#def1"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is identically zero on the complement of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              = succeed ".)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo +
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g + H
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}(G)
            denote the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} TVS
            of Schwartz-Bruhat functions on an abelian Lie group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi AbLie
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi TVS
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}(-): AbLie^{op} \to TVS
          %p
            Finally, the character group of a compactly generated locally compact abelian group is an abelian Lie group. By applying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontryagin+duality"} Pontryagin duality
            to the statement that a locally compact abelian group is canonically a filtered colimit of compactly generated subgroups, we see that any locally compact abelian group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is canonically an inverse limit of a cofiltered diagram of abelian Lie groups
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_\alpha
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mo ⟵
                    %mi α
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi α
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \cong \underset{\longleftarrow}{\lim}_\alpha G_\alpha.
          %p
            We may apply the contravariant functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}(-)
            to this cofiltered diagram to produce a filtered diagram of Schwartz-Bruhat spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}(G_\alpha)
            of abelian Lie groups. In this notation,
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              For a locally compact abelian group
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}(G)
              is the colimit of the filtered diagram of spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}(G_\alpha)
              defined according to Definition
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#def2"}
          %p
            In other words, a Schwartz-Bruhat function on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is one that factors through one of its Lie quotients as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ↠
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi α
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi g
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \twoheadrightarrow G_\alpha \stackrel{g}{\to} \mathbb{C}
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi α
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: G_\alpha \to \mathbb{C}
            is Schwartz-Bruhat in the sense given for Lie groups, Definition
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#def2"}
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The extension of Schwartz functions and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tempered+distributions"} tempered distributions
            on Euclidean spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            to more general locally compact abelian groups was given by Bruhat:
          %ul
            %li
              F. Bruhat,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Distributions sur un groupe localement compact et applications à l’étude des représentations des groupes p-adiques
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/article/BSMF_1961__89__43_0.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            References to the fact that Schwartz-Bruhat spaces can be presented as direct limits of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+spaces"} topological vector spaces
            frequently appear in the literature, e.g.,
          %ul
            %li
              A. Wawrzyńczyk,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On tempered distributions and Bochner-Schwartz theorem on arbitrary locally compact Abelian groups
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://eudml.org/doc/263023"} link
          %p (However, the precise categorical details seem to be hard to come by, or at least treated in somewhat cavalier fashion.)
          %p Some useful background material on the structure of locally compact Hausdorff abelian groups used in the description above can be found here:
          %ul
            %li
              Dikran Dikranjan, Luchezar Stoyanov,
              = succeed "," do
                %em An elementary approach to Haar integration and Pontryagin duality in locally compact abelian groups
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://ac.els-cdn.com/S0166864111002604/1-s2.0-S0166864111002604-main.pdf?_tid=b5b2e8fe-134e-11e8-b733-00000aab0f26&acdnat=1518809121_303788c33fd1d38a829198fb1dd2741a"} pdf
